            id: 8,
            title: "Express Yourself",
            subtitle: "Unit 8 — Std 7 English",
            reading: [
                "Let's Talk: I send a message with my phone, Even when I'm all alone. A computer helps me type and chat, I even video call my cat! TV gives me news each day, While radios talk in their own way. Letters travel slow, but sweet, With stamps and names so nice and neat. All these ways to talk and share, Show someone, somewhere, we care!",
                "How the Camel got his Hump: In the beginning of the world, everything was new. The animals lived in harmony with humans, working together. There was Camel who lived alone in the desert because he didn't like doing any work. He ate dry sticks and thorns. He responded to anyone who spoke to him with just one word: 'Humph!' On a Monday morning, Horse came by with a saddle on his back and said, 'Camel, O Camel! Come, work like the rest of us.' But Camel only said, 'Humph!' So the Horse left. Next, Dog arrived carrying a stick. He asked Camel to fetch and carry loads like the others. Again, Camel just said, 'Humph!' and Dog walked away. Finally, Ox came and said, 'Camel, O Camel! Come and plough the land.' Camel replied with the same 'Humph!' and Ox also left. Camel refused to work so the other animals had to do extra chores until Thursday. Tired and frustrated, they called a meeting near the desert's edge. Camel showed up, still chewing his cud. He laughed at them, said 'Humph!' again, and walked off. The animals decided to complain to the Djinn, who was in charge of all deserts. Horse explained, 'There is a creature in the desert with a long neck and long legs. He has been lazy since Monday.' The Djinn agreed that this was unacceptable and promised to take action. The Djinn found Camel near an oasis, staring at his reflection and eating grass. He asked Camel why he refused to work while others were doing more because of him. But Camel only replied, 'Humph!' So the Djinn waved his hand, and suddenly, Camel's back puffed up into a big hump. Camel asked what had happened. The Djinn explained. Camel hadn't worked for three days. The hump would store food for three days. Now Camel could go without eating for three days and still work. From that day on, Camel worked like the others — and he still carries that hump today.",
                "Sounds and Smiles: We speak with words, we laugh and sing, Kind voices make the friendship ring. A soft 'hello,' a cheerful tone, Can make us feel we're not alone. We look with eyes, we smile and grin, Our faces show what's deep within. A happy face or frown can say, What words sometimes just can't display. A little hug, a high five too, A pat can say, 'I care for you.' When someone holds your hand just right, It makes your heart feel warm and light. We share a snack, we taste and munch, We laugh out loud while we eat lunch. A smell of cake or yummy treat, Can bring back times that were so sweet. We talk in ways, not just with sound, In looks, in touch, and smells around. With all our senses, we connect, To show our love and give respect!",
                "Gulliver in Lilliput: Gulliver was a sailor. He loved adventures. One morning, a terrible storm broke Gulliver's ship into pieces. He swam hard and reached the shore, exhausted and fell asleep on the sand. When he woke up, he could not move. His arms, legs and even his hair were tied to the ground with many tiny ropes. He saw people around him who were only six inches tall. They had bright eyes. They wore red, yellow, and blue clothes with tiny hats. They carried small swords that looked like pins. Gulliver was astonished to see such little beings, brave and standing like soldiers. These were the Lilliputians. Suddenly, he felt movement on his chest. The tiny people were climbing over him. They spoke quickly in a language he did not know. They were pointing at him and waving their arms. Gulliver raised his hands carefully to show he meant no harm. The Lilliputians paused, looked at one another and nodded. They understood he was peaceful. Soon Gulliver grew hungry. He touched his stomach and made a soft sound. The Lilliputians realized he needed food. Some of them ran off and returned with baskets of tiny loaves of bread and pots of milk. It was enough for their families, but only a mouthful for Gulliver. They placed the food near his face. At first, they stepped back in fear as he opened his mouth wide. But Gulliver smiled, picked up the food gently and began to eat. Then they started to talk in their language. 'Look, he is eating our bread,' said one. 'He likes it!' said another. 'Bring more! He is very big,' shouted the third. Gulliver nodded in thanks and pointed to the food and his mouth. The little people laughed and hurried to fetch more. Soon he had eaten hundreds of their loaves and drunk many pots of milk. Afterward, Gulliver rubbed his hands together, smiled and bowed his head. The Lilliputians clapped joyfully. They saw he was friendly and safe."
            ],
