                "Feelings: These things make me cheerful - Sunshine on the sea, Birthday parties, presents, And my favourite food with tea. These things make me sad - A grey and gloomy day, Unkind words and unkind looks When friends just walk away. These things make me angry - Pets who pull my hair, People who break promises, And times when life's not fair. These things make me frightened - Thunderstorms that BOOM! Crawly bugs and creepy dreams And shadows round my room. These things make me calm - A smile from a friend, Sleepy bedtime stories With a very happy end.",
                "Sushrut: The Father of Surgery: Sushrut was a bright student of veterinary medicine. His teacher, Shalihotra, once decided to test him. He asked Sushrut, 'What would you require to perform a surgery on a horse?' Sushrut, very quickly listed down all the instruments and the medicines that would be needed. 'Three things are missing.' Shalihotra said. Confused, Sushrut looked at him. 'I will give you a week to find them.' Shalihotra consoled. Curious to find out what those three things were, Sushrut observed his teacher carefully during this time. Before operating the horse, Shalihotra observed its every body part. Right then Sushrut found the first missing thing! 'A good veterinarian should have an eagle's eye.' Then Shalihotra treated the animal as if it were his own; balancing science and compassion equally. And lo! Sushrut found his second rule. 'A good veterinarian should have a lion's heart.' Shalihotra helped the horse recover after the surgery. Sushrut came to know that 'A good veterinarian should have a mother's touch too.' Later in his life, Sushrut got renowned as the 'Father of Surgery' and wrote a book on surgery!",
                "Happy Hearts: Happy hearts feel bright and free, Joyful moments bring energy. Kind words spoken spread love and light, Confidence gives strength to fight. Happy vibes help dreams take flight, Joy in laughter makes the heart light. Kindness touches souls so dear, Confidence boosts courage year by year.",
                "The Rainbow of Feelings: It was a warm afternoon. Class 7 students gathered in the hall for a special session with their counsellor, Devrajbhai. The children sat quietly, curious about what they were going to learn. Devrajbhai looked around and smiled. 'Today, we will talk about our feelings. Feelings are like colours. Sometimes they are bright, sometimes they are dark. Now, who wants to share about feelings?' Riya raised her hand first. 'Sir, I feel jealous when my friend Aisha gets new crayons or higher marks. My heart feels heavy.' Devrajbhai nodded gently. 'Jealousy comes when we only look at what others have. Instead, think of your own gifts. Say, I am happy for myself and for my friends. Try clapping for others when they do well.' Next, Arjun spoke, 'Sir, I often feel greedy. I always want more toys and the biggest share of snacks.' Devrajbhai explained, 'Greed is a trick our brain plays. Before asking for anything, pause and say, Do I really need this? Try sharing. Giving also brings joy. Say: I have enough.' Then Nisha stood up slowly. 'Sir, I feel inferior. When others do better, I think I am useless.' Devrajbhai shook his head kindly. 'No child is useless. Everyone is unique. Write one good thing about yourself every day. Celebrate small successes. Instead of comparing, learn from others.' Imran added nervously, 'Sir, I feel stress before exams. I cannot sleep, and I even get a stomachache.' Devrajbhai said, 'Stress is your body's way of saying, I am worried.' To calm down, take deep breaths, walk in nature, listen to music, or talk to family. You will feel lighter.' Finally, Meena whispered, 'Sir, I am shy. I never answer in the class.' Devrajbhai smiled warmly. 'Shyness is natural. Start with small steps. Speak one line in the class, practice in front of a mirror, join a group game. Slowly, you will grow confident.' He looked at all the children. 'Your feelings are not your enemies. They are messages. Thank your feelings, but guide them wisely. Remember, you are stronger than you think.' The children clapped. They felt calmer, like a fresh breeze had touched their hearts. They promised to reset their feelings with gratitude, sharing, and practice."
